"Source: harmless_supplement.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Title: Medicines for Metabolic Conditions\nMetabolic conditions such as diabetes and obesity are managed with a range of lifestyle measures and medicines, and new treatments regularly attract attention from patients and clinicians. Discussions often cover adherence, side effects, cost and access, and how therapies fit alongside diet and exercise. Coverage may also note supply constraints and off-label interest. This general context does not state any drug's molecular mechanism or its specific approved indications.\n\n\nSource: normal_sources.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Source: Wikipedia, \"GLP-1 receptor agonist\" - CC BY-SA - retrieved 2026-09-14\nURL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GLP-1_receptor_agonist\nGLP-1 receptor agonists \"mimic the actions of the endogenous incretin hormone GLP-1.\"\n\"GLP-1 receptor activation slows gastric emptying, inhibits the release of glucagon\" and increases insulin secretion; it \"stimulates satiety, thus reducing food intake.\"\n\"semaglutide (Ozempic and Rybelsus for diabetes, Wegovy for weight management ...).\"\n\nSource: MacDonald et al. review, NCBI PMC - retrieved 2026-09-14 - peer-reviewed\nURL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3556522/\n\"Stimulation of insulin secretion by GLP-1R agonists is glucose-dependent.\" Source: harmless_supplement.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Title: Medicines for Metabolic Conditions\nMetabolic conditions such as diabetes and obesity are managed with a range of lifestyle measures and medicines, and new treatments regularly attract attention from patients and clinicians. Discussions often cover adherence, side effects, cost and access, and how therapies fit alongside diet and exercise. Coverage may also note supply constraints and off-label interest. This general context does not state any drug's molecular mechanism or its specific approved indications.\n\n\nSource: normal_sources.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Source: Wikipedia, \"GLP-1 receptor agoni